# `print::[T]` and `render::[T]` coerce their argument to the explicit target
# type first, exactly like `let tmp: T = x; print tmp` -- the same coercion
# `copy::[T]` already applies to its own argument. `render::[json]` renders a
# text value as a *quoted* JSON string even when `quote_strings = false`,
# because `quote_strings` only affects rendering the value at its OWN
# (unwrapped) type; once coerced to json, the value is JSON, not text.
param label: text

# No explicit target: unchanged baseline (text prints/renders bare).
print(label)
print(render(label, quote_strings = false))

# Explicit `::[json]` target: a text argument coerces to json, so both `print`
# and `render` quote/escape it -- even `render(..., quote_strings = false)`,
# since `quote_strings` no longer applies once the value is json.
print::[json](label)
print(render::[json](label, quote_strings = false))

# `copy::[json]` already coerces (pre-existing behavior); printing its result
# must match `print::[json]` exactly, pinning that both paths agree.
print(copy::[json](label))

# Scalar widening target: int -> decimal. A whole-number decimal renders the
# same as the originating int: no coercion failure, no unexpected formatting.
print::[decimal](7)

# An explicit target equal to the argument's own natural type is a no-op.
print::[text](label)
